.PP
Initialize the Repository Store for the current Git repository.
.PP
Stores live in platform\-local user data outside the checkout. Stop all tk processes before running init to migrate a legacy Store from Git metadata. Init repairs uniquely identified Vacant Stores; other broken associations print ranked evidence and complete recovery commands. Attachment requires valid metadata and a readable database. Release the former repository\*(Aqs pointer before attaching a Store it still owns. Both recovery options refuse a healthy association and preserve prior data.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
\fBArguments and options\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-attach\fR \fI<STORE_ID>\fR
Explicitly attach a valid Store whose former ownership has been released
.TP
\fB\-\-new\fR
Create a distinct Store, preserving all prior Stores
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.PP
The Repository Store is a SQLite database at
\fI<local data>/tk/stores/<Store ID>/tk.db\fR. The local data root is the
platform-local user data directory: XDG data home on Linux, Application Support
on macOS, and LocalAppData on Windows. All linked Workspaces share the Store.
It survives deletion of the repository until the user explicitly deletes it.
The repository-local \fItk.storeId\fR and the Store's \fIstore.json\fR must
agree on its identity and Git Common Directory association.
.PP
Ordinary commands read Git metadata without writing it or refreshing the manifest.
They still need a writable Store directory for SQLite and lock files. Sandboxes
must grant the specific Store directory as a writable root. Initialization and
reassociation also write Git config and the data-root initialization lock.
.PP
For a legacy Store under \fI<git-common-dir>/tk/\fR, stop all tk processes,
then run \fBtk init\fR. Keep old binaries stopped through retries and cleanup.
Init moves a consistent SQLite image and all Store Backups into
\fI<local data>/tk/stores/<Store ID>/\fR and reports both locations and the ID.
Ordinary commands direct you to init; Prime stays silent.
.PP
If migration stops before installing the Git pointer, the legacy Store remains
authoritative. Run init again: it retains the Store ID and rebuilds the image
from the current legacy data. After pointer installation, the durable Store is
authoritative and retry only finishes cleanup. If legacy files changed after
cutover, tk preserves both Stores and refuses cleanup; restore manually.
Do not remove \fItk-migration.json\fR or \fImigration.json\fR to bypass a refusal.
Unknown files, invalid metadata, and unsafe or busy Stores require inspection.
.PP
File contents are flushed on every platform; directory entries are also flushed
on Unix. Process-interruption recovery is supported; Windows sudden-power-loss
safety is not established by this protocol. On Unix, hard-linked legacy databases
are refused: replace the hard links with independent copies before retrying.
Attach and new refuse legacy data and pending migrations.
.PP
With a broken association, plain init can repair a uniquely identified Vacant
Store after confirming release of its former ownership. The candidate must match
the referenced Store ID or current canonical Git Common Directory; Remote URLs
and historical paths alone do not authorize repair. Its database and all retained
Store Backups must contain no user-owned data beyond initialization defaults.
Unreadable or incompletely inspected contents require explicit recovery.
Otherwise plain init prints ranked Store evidence and complete recovery commands.
Use \fBtk init \-\-attach\fR \fIStore-ID\fR to attach a valid,
readable Store after its former repository releases ownership. Use
\fBtk init \-\-new\fR to create a distinct Store while preserving prior Stores.
Both options refuse healthy associations and cannot be combined. A missing
referenced Store is reported as possible data loss; its ID is never recreated.
.PP
Unreadable or invalid metadata requires manual restoration. Failed ownership
inspection never permits attachment. If the former Git Common Directory's parent
is missing, restore access to that parent first; it may be an unavailable volume.
An active command holds the Store's association lock until it closes the database,
so attachment may ask you to retry. After an interrupted attachment, retry the
same attach command; if Git already installed the pointer, plain init confirms it.
.PP
.B tk init
is idempotent. If the store already exists and is current, it reports the
existing path instead of recreating it.
.PP
Opening a store created by an older
.B tk
applies the pending schema migrations first. Before doing so,
.B tk
writes a compacted copy of the store to
\fI<local data>/tk/stores/<Store ID>/backups/\fR and keeps the ten most recent. If that copy
cannot be written, the migration is refused and the store is left untouched.
.PP
Restoring a backup does not reverse an upgrade: the current binary re-applies
the same migrations the next time it opens the store. To recover a value a migration lost, read it out of
the backup with an external
.BR sqlite3 (1)
\(em which
.B tk
does not ship \(em and re-apply it through the normal commands. If a backup is
copied over
\fItk.db\fR anyway, delete \fItk.db\-wal\fR and \fItk.db\-shm\fR first; the
copy is not in WAL mode, and the store stays out of WAL until
.B tk init
runs again.
