.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
Select the next ready Ticket
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
\fBArguments and options\fR
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print only the bare Display ID, omitting the title
.TP
\fB\-\-plan\fR
Select only Plan members, intersecting any Epic Scope
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fIEPIC_ID\fR]
Restrict selection to this Epic\*(Aqs child Tickets. Falls back to the `TK_SCOPE` environment variable; absent both, all ready Tickets are considered
.PP
.B tk next
selects open Tickets with no unresolved Dependencies or External Blockers. It
orders ready Tickets by Effective Priority, then own Priority, then creation
order in the Repository Store, and never selects Epics. Effective Priority is
the lowest of a candidate's own Priority and the Priorities of every unfinished
item it transitively blocks through Dependencies and Epic Membership, so a ready
Ticket that gates a higher-Priority Blocked Item outranks lower-priority
direct work. Effective Priority does not cross the Scope boundary, so the same
Ticket can rank differently scoped and unscoped.
Backend Tickets use local import order to break ties, not Backend creation
time. Ticket Kind does not affect ordering.
.PP
.B tk next
does not explain skipped candidates. When the selected Ticket's Effective
Priority is lower than its own Priority,
.B tk next
writes one rationale line to standard error in the form
.RS 4
.nf
<display-id>: Effective Priority <P> (via <contributor-id>)
.fi
.RE
so the non-obvious pick is explainable without a follow-up
.BR "tk show" .
Standard output is a single line carrying the Display ID and the title.
.B \-q
.RB ( \-\-quiet )
prints the Display ID alone, so
.B id=$(tk next \-q)
captures only the selection; redirect standard error with
.B 2>/dev/null
to suppress the rationale, which
.B \-q
does not itself silence.
.PP
Parent Epic status, Dependencies, and External Blockers do not hide otherwise
ready child Tickets.
.PP
Mutation Log, Mutation Failure, and Sync Cursor state do not filter
.B tk next
selection, and
.B tk next
does not emit sync-health warnings.
.PP
.B tk next
is read-only and does not change Item Status. Use
.B tk start
to begin work on the selected Ticket.
.PP
An optional positional
.I epic-id
scopes selection to that Epic and its child Tickets, falling back to
.B TK_SCOPE
when the argument is absent. Absent both,
.B tk next
considers every ready Ticket in the Repository Store. A Ticket supplied where
an Epic is expected is a failure, not a single-item selection.
.PP
.B tk next
answers with one Ticket. To inspect the whole ready set instead, use
.BR "tk list --ready" .
.PP
On success,
.B tk next
prints one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<display-id>: <title>
.fi
.RE
.PP
With
.BR \-q ,
that line is the bare Display ID, unstyled whatever the colour policy:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<display-id>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B tk next
has no JSON or structured-output mode in v1; the
.B \-q
Display ID line is the machine interface.
.PP
If no ready Ticket matches,
.B tk next
returns exit status 1 and reports on standard error that nothing is ready,
naming the Epic when a Scope was active. A Scope that names no known item, or
names a Ticket rather than an Epic, fails the same way without selecting
anything.
.PP
.B \-\-plan
restricts selection to the current Plan. An Epic argument or
.B TK_SCOPE
intersects that membership; the argument still wins over the environment.
Effective Priority stops at the combined boundary. Outside Dependencies still
block readiness, but their Items are never included or selected automatically.
An empty result names the Plan and any Epic Scope. Without
.BR \-\-plan ,
Plan membership does not narrow selection.
