.SS "tk grep"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.nf
tk grep [OPTIONS] <PATTERN>
.fi
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
Search title and body text for a regular expression; show context by default
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
\fBArguments and options\fR
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fR
Match case\-insensitively (full Unicode case folding)
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-fixed\-strings\fR
Match the pattern as a literal string, not a regular expression
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show N lines of context on each side of a match (default 3)
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-after\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show N lines of context after each match (overrides \-C)
.TP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-before\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show N lines of context before each match (overrides \-C)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress all output and exit on the first match
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR
Print the count of matching items instead of the match blocks
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR
Print each match as `<display\-id>: <title>`
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIPATTERN\fR>
Regular expression to search for in title and body text
.PP
Find Tickets and Epics whose title or body matches the regular expression
.I PATTERN
and, by default, render each match as a
.BR "tk show" -style
block with the body collapsed to the matching lines plus surrounding context.
.PP
Grep covers the whole Repository Store and every Item Status, including done;
like
.B tk search
it ignores Scope and is never narrowed by TK_SCOPE. It searches title and body
text \(em content search, as opposed to the title-only lookup of
.B tk search
or the exact Display ID / Alias lookup of
.BR tk\ show .
.PP
.I PATTERN
is a Rust regular expression (RE2-family, like \fBgrep \-E\fR; no
backreferences or lookaround) and matching is case-sensitive by default. Matched
text is highlighted on a terminal. Matches print in creation order, never ranked,
with three lines of context each side by default,
.B \-\-
between non-contiguous hunks within one item, and a blank line between items.
An empty pattern, or an invalid regular expression, is a usage error
(a whitespace pattern is a valid needle, matched like \fBgrep\fR).
.PP
.B \-C
sets the context window to
.I N
lines each side (default 3);
.B \-A
and
.B \-B
override the trailing and leading side independently, and
.B "\-C 0"
shows only the matching lines.
.PP
.B \-q
suppresses all output and exits on the first match;
.B \-c
prints the count of matching items instead of the blocks.
.B \-l
or
.B \-\-list
prints one plain
.B "<display-id>: <title>"
line per matching Item. A title and body with several matching lines still
produce one line. The list form includes Epics and done Items, prints in
creation order, and ignores TK_SCOPE like the default form. It accepts
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-F ,
and the context flags; context flags have no effect. It is unstyled whatever
the colour policy, and omits all metadata, including status, kind, priority,
dates, Mutation markers, and Pending Promotion labels or Binding rows.
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-l
are mutually exclusive. All three keep the
exit-status predicate described below.
.PP
.B tk grep
overloads its exit status as a \fBgrep \-q\fR\-style predicate: it exits 0 when
at least one item matches and 1 when none do, leaving stderr empty so a script
can tell \(lqno match\(rq from a real failure. A usage error exits 2.
.PP
By default, on success it prints one
.B tk show
\-style block per matching item:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
○ project-2 · Add middleware
  P2 · Task · Created: 2026-06-04
  the handler validates the auth token
.fi
.RE
