.el .ds Aq '
.PP
Adopt an existing Backend issue into the local working set
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
\fBArguments and options\fR
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIKEY\fR>
Backend\-native key of the issue to adopt (a bare issue number for GitHub). Passed to the Backend Adapter verbatim — tk does not normalise URLs or `#`\-prefixes before Adapter canonicalization
.PP
Adopt fetches one existing issue through the configured Remote. A new
Backend Ticket starts accepted at Priority P2. Adoption records the current
Backend state without queuing a Mutation.
.PP
If the Backend identity belongs to an Item previously detached, Re-Adopt
binds that same Item again and takes the Backend's current Shared Fields.
It preserves Priority, Selection State, and Plan membership. A Backend close
clears Work State; a Backend reopen clears an obsolete Closing Reason.
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS "tk detach"
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.nf
tk detach <ID>
.fi
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
Keep a Backend Item as local work without changing the Backend object
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.PP
\fBArguments and options\fR
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIID\fR>
Display ID or Alias of the Backend Item to detach
.PP
Detach keeps the Item and its Local Fields, including Plan membership,
while removing its Backend Binding. It restores the former local Display ID
when known, otherwise assigns a new local Display ID. The Backend Display ID
stops resolving; the Former Backend Identity remains available for Re-Adopt.
.PP
Detach reaches no Backend and leaves the Backend object unchanged. It
withdraws pending or failed Mutations that need the removed identity.
It refuses a Pending Promotion: use
.B tk promote cancel
to withdraw that operation instead. It also refuses if a Backend-bound
Blocked Item would wait on the detached Local Item; remove that Dependency
with
.B tk unblock
first.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Logical failure, such as missing Repository Store or invalid message input.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Unexpected internal error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TK_SCOPE
Supplies a Scope to
.B tk next
and
.BR "tk list" .
The value is the Display ID or Alias of an Epic. A positional
.I epic-id
argument overrides it; unset or empty means no Scope.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Any non-empty value suppresses styled output.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Any non-empty value forces styled output even when the stream is not a
terminal.
.PP
Styling is resolved separately for standard output and standard error, so
piping one stream does not silence the other.
.B NO_COLOR
wins over
.BR CLICOLOR_FORCE ,
which wins over terminal detection; a stream that is not a terminal is
unstyled by default. A terminal that cannot render SGR escape sequences
receives plain text rather than literal escape bytes.
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize a repository-local store:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk init
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a local Ticket from a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add -F followup.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a local Ticket from standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add -F - <<'EOF'
Investigate flaky login retry

Repro is intermittent on the staging cluster.
First seen 2026-04-30.
EOF
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create an Epic and a child Ticket:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk add --epic -m "Jira backend"
tk add --parent project-1 -m "Map Jira issue fields"
.fi
.RE
.PP
List visible work:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk list
tk list --ready
.fi
.RE
.PP
Narrow the same views to one Epic and its child Tickets:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk list project-1
tk next project-1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select the next ready Ticket:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk next
.fi
.RE
.PP
Work through one Epic without restating it on every command. Exporting the
Scope keeps it in the environment, so it survives across a long unattended
run:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
export TK_SCOPE=project-1
while id=$(tk next \-q); do
    tk start "$id"
    # ... implement ...
    tk done "$id"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print agent guidance:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tk prime
.fi
.RE
